WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.333
Conditional probability.

2
00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:06.970
A language model predicts the next token given all the previous ones.

3
00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.233
That word, given, is the whole idea.

4
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:13.033
Conditional probability is a simple move.

5
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:14.767
You learn that B happened.

6
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.833
So you throw away every outcome where B did not.

7
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:21.500
Then you re-measure how likely A is, inside what is left.

8
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.733
Here are all thirty six outcomes of rolling two dice, showing their sums.

9
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:29.734
Now suppose we learn the first die is three.

10
00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:32.067
Only this row of six outcomes remains.

11
00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:35.469
Inside that row, just one cell has a sum of eight.

12
00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:39.067
So the probability of eight, given a three, is one in six.

13
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:41.500
Here is the formula.

14
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.033
On top, the overlap: cases where both A and B happen.

15
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:48.367
Underneath, the new universe, which is everything in B.

16
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:52.100
So the conditional probability is the overlap's share of B.

17
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.167
We can check this by simulation.

18
00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.867
Roll two dice a hundred thousand times.

19
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:00.667
Keep only the rolls where the first die is three.

20
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:03.182
And measure how often the sum is eight.

21
00:01:03.182 --> 00:01:05.000
It comes out close to one sixth.

22
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.933
Two cautions before moving on.

23
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:10.359
Conditioning is not causation.

24
00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:16.100
A can be likely given B because B causes A, A causes B, or something causes both.

25
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.167
And if the probability does not change when you condition, the events are independent.

26
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:23.700
Here is a real case.

27
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Your agent reads flare events and is asked about a geomagnetic storm this weekend.

28
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.200
The honest answer conditions on the evidence, a fresh M class flare.

29
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.933
Quote the unconditional base rate when evidence exists, and the answer is confidently useless.

30
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:40.888
Let's recap.

31
00:01:40.888 --> 00:01:44.033
Conditional probability shrinks the universe to B.

32
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:47.767
It is the overlap divided by the probability of B.

33
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:52.007
If it equals the plain probability, the events are independent.

34
00:01:52.007 --> 00:01:53.633
It never proves causation.

35
00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:55.833
Next up: random variables.
